\section{s:typed-effects}{Typed algebraic effects}
%HEVEA\cutname{typedeffects.html}
\ikwd{effect\@\texttt{effect}}

(Introduced in OCaml 5.6)

\begin{syntax}
typexpr:
      ...
    | typexpr '->' typexpr
    | typexpr '-[' [effect-row] ']->' typexpr
;
effect-row:
      effect-fields [ '|' effect-row-tail ]
    | '>' effect-fields
    | effect-row-tail
;
effect-fields:
      constr-name { ',' constr-name }
;
effect-row-tail:
      "'" ident
    | [type-params] typeconstr-name
;
typedef:
      ...
    | [type-params] typeconstr-name '=' '-[' [effect-row] ']-'
;
str-item:
      ...
    | 'effect' eff-name [ '=' '-[' [effect-row] ']-' ]
;
sig-item:
      ...
    | 'effect' eff-name [ '=' '-[' [effect-row] ']-' ]
;
mod-constraint:
      ...
    | 'effect' eff-name '=' '-[' [effect-row] ']-'
    | 'effect' eff-name ':=' '-[' [effect-row] ']-'
;
pattern:
      ...
    | 'effect' pattern ',' value-name
;
\end{syntax}

Since OCaml 5.6, the language features a static effect typing system built on row
polymorphism. The system tracks effect perform operations at compile time,
ensuring that effectful operations are statically documented and soundly handled,
while preserving full backward compatibility with standard OCaml code.

\subsection{s:typed-effects-principles}{Core Principles}

\subsubsection{s:typed-effects-pure-default}{Purity by Default}

The standard function arrow "->" is defined as completely pure ($\emptyset$,
closed empty row). Standard library definitions and existing user code without
effect annotations infer pure arrow types:

\begin{verbatim}
val add : int -> int -> int
val map_pure : ('a -> 'b) -> 'a list -> 'b list
\end{verbatim}

Computations that perform algebraic effects via "Effect.perform" require
latent effect annotations on the arrows. An arrow of the form
"t1 -[ E1, E2 | 'e ]-> t2" denotes a function that takes an argument of type
"t1", produces a result of type "t2", and may perform effects "E1", "E2",
as well as any effects contained in the row tail "'e".

The syntax "-[ > E1, E2 ]-" is syntactic sugar for an anonymous open row with
a fresh, unconstrained row tail.

\subsubsection{s:typed-effects-subsumption}{Bidirectional Effect Subsumption}

The type system implements bidirectional effect subsumption based on row
inclusion ($R_1 \subseteq R_2$). A function with fewer effects may be passed
directly to a context expecting wider effects without requiring explicit
coercion syntax (":>"):

\begin{verbatim}
type _ Effect.t += Yield : unit Effect.t | Log : string -> unit Effect.t

let f_pure () = ()
let f_yield () = Effect.perform Yield

(* Pure function subsumed into effectful list *)
let tasks : (unit -[ Yield ]-> unit) list = [ f_pure; f_yield ]

(* Pure callback subsumed into effectful higher-order parameter *)
let run (worker : unit -[ Yield, Log ]-> unit) = ...
let () = run f_pure
\end{verbatim}

Subsumption operates symmetrically across data structures (lists, tuples,
records) and function arguments during type-directed checking.

\subsection{s:typed-effects-handlers}{Handlers and Row Subtraction}

When handling effects via deep or shallow pattern matching ("match ... with effect ..."),
the handled effect constructors are subtracted from the computation's latent effect
row ($R \setminus \{\ell\}$):

\begin{verbatim}
type _ Effect.t += Yield : unit Effect.t

(* Yield is handled; remaining computation is purely unit -> int *)
let run_pure () : int =
  match 42 with
  | v -> v
  | effect Yield, k -> Effect.Deep.continue k ()

(* Handler on open computation subtracts Yield, propagating 'e *)
let run_rigid (type e) (f : unit -[ Yield | e ]-> int) : int =
  match f () with
  | v -> v
  | effect Yield, k -> Effect.Deep.continue k ()
\end{verbatim}

The handler construct guarantees that handling a set of effects does not force
handled labels onto pure enclosing scopes, preventing spurious effect leakage.

\subsection{s:typed-effects-modules}{Modular Effects and Abstract Signatures}

Signatures support abstract and manifest effect row specifications via the
"effect" keyword:

\begin{verbatim}
module type RUNNER = sig
  effect eff
  val step : unit -[ eff ]-> bool
end
\end{verbatim}

Modules can satisfy this signature by defining concrete effect equations:

\begin{verbatim}
module YieldRunner : (RUNNER with effect eff = -[ Yield ]-) = struct
  effect eff = -[ Yield ]-
  let step () = Effect.perform Yield; true
end
\end{verbatim}

Destructive substitution ("with effect eff := ...") removes the abstract
effect declaration from the signature and inlines the concrete row directly into
all occurrences. Substituting an empty row "-[ ]-" collapses the arrows down to
standard pure arrows:

\begin{verbatim}
module PureRunner : (RUNNER with effect eff := -[ ]-) = struct
  let step () = true
end
(* Resulting signature: sig val step : unit -> bool end *)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{s:typed-effects-polymorphism}{Higher-Order Combinators and Polymorphic Recursion}

Higher-order functions that accept effectful callbacks parameterize over the
callback's effect row using explicit polymorphism:

\begin{verbatim}
let rec map : 'a 'b 'e. ('a -['e]-> 'b) -> 'a list -['e]-> 'b list =
  fun f -> function
  | [] -> []
  | x :: xs ->
      let y = f x in
      y :: map f xs
\end{verbatim}

When "map" is applied to a pure callback, the row variable "'e" instantiates to
empty, yielding a completely pure function "'a list -> 'b list".

For extensible GADT effect constructors carrying existential effect callbacks,
the type system automatically widens the existential effect row into the
enclosing polymorphic scope during pattern matching:

\begin{verbatim}
type _ Effect.t += Fork : 'e. (unit -['e]-> unit) -> unit Effect.t

let rec run_all : 'e. (unit -['e]-> unit) list -> unit = function
  | [] -> ()
  | task :: rest ->
      let spawned = ref [] in
      (match task () with
       | () -> ()
       | effect (Fork child), k ->
           spawned := child :: !spawned;
           Effect.Deep.continue k ());
      run_all (!spawned @ rest)
\end{verbatim}
